WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.711
Reducing extraneous load means removing presentation friction that does not support learning.

2
00:00:04.711 --> 00:00:10.991
Cluttered slides, decorative graphics, unnecessary detail, weak examples, unclear labels, and poor pacing all consume attention.

3
00:00:10.991 --> 00:00:18.842
Learners may still work hard, but their effort goes into sorting through the material rather than understanding the core relationship.

4
00:00:18.842 --> 00:00:22.375
Clean design is not about making a course plain.

5
00:00:22.375 --> 00:00:25.515
It is about making the important structure visible.

6
00:00:25.515 --> 00:00:29.048
Split attention is a common source of extraneous load.

7
00:00:29.048 --> 00:00:40.040
It happens when learners must divide attention between poorly integrated sources of information, such as a diagram on one part of the page and the explanation somewhere else.

8
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.143
The learner has to search, match, and remember pieces before understanding can begin.

9
00:00:45.143 --> 00:00:52.601
Integrating labels, placing explanations close to the relevant visual, and aligning narration with the diagram can reduce that burden.

10
00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:54.564
Redundancy can also create load.

11
00:00:54.564 --> 00:01:01.630
Repeating the same information in unnecessary forms may seem helpful, but it can force learners to process clutter.

12
00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:09.480
For example, reading dense on-screen text word for word while the learner sees the same text can become inefficient.

13
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.013
The issue is not that repetition is always bad.

14
00:01:13.013 --> 00:01:18.116
The issue is whether each representation adds value or merely competes for attention.

15
00:01:18.116 --> 00:01:20.472
Navigation and pacing matter as well.

16
00:01:20.472 --> 00:01:32.641
If learners are uncertain where to click, what section they are in, or how examples relate to the current concept, working memory is spent on the interface instead of the idea.

17
00:01:32.641 --> 00:01:44.809
Strong materials help learners focus on the essential relationship: what is being explained, why it matters, how the parts connect, and what the learner should be able to do with it.
